### What are the core entities?

The core entities are `Movie`, `Cinema`, `Screen`, `Seat`, `Show`, `SeatStatus`, and `Booking`.

```java
package com.bank.booking.model;

public enum SeatStatus {
    AVAILABLE, LOCKED, BOOKED
}
```

```java
package com.bank.booking.model;

public class Seat {
    private final String seatId;
    private SeatStatus status;
    private String lockedByUserId;
    private long lockExpiry;

    public Seat(String seatId) {
        this.seatId = seatId;
        this.status = SeatStatus.AVAILABLE;
    }

    public synchronized boolean lock(String userId, long ttlMillis) {
        if (status != SeatStatus.AVAILABLE) return false;
        this.status = SeatStatus.LOCKED;
        this.lockedByUserId = userId;
        this.lockExpiry = System.currentTimeMillis() + ttlMillis;
        return true;
    }

    public synchronized boolean confirm(String userId) {
        if (status != SeatStatus.LOCKED
                || !userId.equals(lockedByUserId)) return false;
        this.status = SeatStatus.BOOKED;
        return true;
    }

    public synchronized void releaseIfExpired() {
        if (status == SeatStatus.LOCKED
                && System.currentTimeMillis() > lockExpiry) {
            this.status = SeatStatus.AVAILABLE;
            this.lockedByUserId = null;
            System.out.println("Seat " + seatId
                    + " lock expired, released");
        }
    }

    public String getSeatId() { return seatId; }
    public SeatStatus getStatus() { return status; }
}
```

### How does the two-phase lock-then-confirm work?

This is the central mechanism that every real booking platform uses.

1. User selects seats → seats transition to LOCKED with a short TTL (e.g., 5 minutes).
2. User completes payment within the TTL → seats transition to BOOKED.
3. If the TTL expires → a background sweeper returns seats to AVAILABLE.

```java
package com.bank.booking.service;

import com.bank.booking.model.*;
import java.util.List;

public class BookingService {
    private static final long LOCK_TTL = 5 * 60 * 1000; // 5 minutes

    public boolean lockSeats(Show show, List<String> seatIds,
                              String userId) {
        for (String seatId : seatIds) {
            Seat seat = show.getSeat(seatId);
            if (!seat.lock(userId, LOCK_TTL)) {
                // Rollback any already-locked seats
                seatIds.stream()
                        .map(show::getSeat)
                        .filter(s -> s.getStatus() == SeatStatus.LOCKED)
                        .forEach(Seat::releaseIfExpired);
                return false;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Locked seats " + seatIds
                + " for user " + userId);
        return true;
    }

    public Booking confirmBooking(Show show, List<String> seatIds,
                                   String userId) {
        for (String seatId : seatIds) {
            if (!show.getSeat(seatId).confirm(userId)) {
                throw new IllegalStateException(
                        "Cannot confirm seat " + seatId);
            }
        }
        Booking booking = new Booking(
                java.util.UUID.randomUUID().toString(),
                show, seatIds, userId);
        System.out.println("Booking confirmed: " + booking.getBookingId());
        return booking;
    }
}
```

### Why must the lock be atomic?

Checking that a seat is available and setting it to locked must happen as one indivisible step. Otherwise two users both see AVAILABLE and both lock the same seat.

```java
// The lock method is synchronized on the Seat object
public synchronized boolean lock(String userId, long ttlMillis) {
    if (status != SeatStatus.AVAILABLE) return false;
    this.status = SeatStatus.LOCKED;
    // ...
}
```

In a distributed deployment, use a Redis lock keyed by show and seat with a TTL:

```text
SET show:S1:seat:A5 userId NX PX 300000
```

The `NX` flag ensures the key is set only if it does not already exist (atomic), and `PX 300000` sets a 5-minute expiry.

### How does the background sweeper work?

A scheduled task periodically scans locked seats and releases any whose TTL has expired.

```java
package com.bank.booking.service;

import com.bank.booking.model.Show;

public class LockSweeper implements Runnable {
    private final Show show;

    public LockSweeper(Show show) {
        this.show = show;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        show.getAllSeats().forEach(
                seat -> seat.releaseIfExpired());
    }
}
```

### How does payment and cancellation work?

Payment goes through a payment strategy. Cancellation releases seats back to AVAILABLE.

```java
public void cancel(Booking booking, Show show) {
    for (String seatId : booking.getSeatIds()) {
        show.getSeat(seatId).release();
    }
    System.out.println("Booking " + booking.getBookingId()
            + " cancelled, seats released");
}
```

### What about the Show entity?

A Show is one screening of a Movie on a Screen at a specific time. It holds the state of each seat.

```java
package com.bank.booking.model;

import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.*;

public class Show {
    private final String showId;
    private final Movie movie;
    private final Screen screen;
    private final LocalDateTime showTime;
    private final Map<String, Seat> seats;

    public Show(String showId, Movie movie, Screen screen,
                LocalDateTime showTime, List<String> seatIds) {
        this.showId = showId;
        this.movie = movie;
        this.screen = screen;
        this.showTime = showTime;
        this.seats = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (String id : seatIds) {
            seats.put(id, new Seat(id));
        }
    }

    public Seat getSeat(String seatId) { return seats.get(seatId); }
    public Collection<Seat> getAllSeats() { return seats.values(); }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The TTL on the lock is core correctness, not optional. Without it, abandoned selections permanently starve inventory.
- In a distributed system, Redis with NX + PX gives both atomicity and automatic expiry.
- The lock-then-confirm pattern is the same two-phase approach used in banking for hold-then-capture on credit cards.
- For popular shows, thousands of users contend for the same seats simultaneously, so the locking mechanism must be highly efficient.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you handle partial lock failure (some seats lock, one fails)?
- What happens if payment takes longer than the TTL?
- How would you implement seat selection UI showing real-time availability?
- How do you handle refunds on cancellation?
- How would you scale this to millions of concurrent users?
